Create an SVG diagram for a technical talk: "2.7 Cloud Hypervisor and KVM", with
the lead line "The VMM is a user-space application - KVM is the privileged
part in the kernel". Keep it simple: one static figure, no task lists.

- The title carries the number of the matching slide heading.
- 16:9 canvas (1200x675), white background.
- Layered view with full-width bands, labelled on the left:
  - "User space" / "unprivileged": two plain app boxes (firefox, rustc) and
    the cloud-hypervisor process box ("VMM - a normal process, one per VM")
    containing a "Guest VM" box ("its own kernel and apps, e.g. Linux or
    Windows").
  - "Kernel space" / "privileged": a "Linux kernel" box ("scheduler, memory,
    drivers, file systems") containing the "KVM" box ("kernel module behind
    /dev/kvm"), placed below cloud-hypervisor.
  - Hardware bar: "Hardware: CPU with virtualization extensions (Intel VMX,
    AMD SVM)". Use the technical names VMX/SVM, not VT-x/AMD-V.
- One arrow from cloud-hypervisor down to KVM, labelled "ioctl()¹" (mono).
- Superscript numbers refer to footnotes rendered by the slide (<Footnotes>),
  not by the SVG. Keep the area below y ~ 530 empty for them.
- Deck style (must match the talk's CSS tokens exactly):
  - Fonts: 'IBM Plex Sans', sans-serif; mono 'Source Code Pro', monospace.
  - Title: fill #8a0404, font-weight 600, letter-spacing -0.035em.
  - Body text #242126, secondary text #706b6d, hairlines #e6e1df.
  - Semantic accents only for boxes: blue (#3b6fd8 / #eaf1ff) VMM, purple
    (#7c4dcc / #f3ebff) KVM, green (#2f9e5b / #e9f7ef) VM/guest, grey
    (#9ca3af / #f9fafb) hardware, host apps and kernel.
